      "knowledge_point": "Simplifying an Expression Using the Alternate Forms of the Secant-Tangent Identity",
      "problem": "Simplify $(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)={sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nThen, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x-1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x=1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)=1.\n$$",
