    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-120652",
      "knowledge_point_id": "eda44360-52ec-59f7-98b4-f8d866a465e9",
      "knowledge_point": "Simplifying an Expression Using the Alternate Forms of the Secant-Tangent Identity",
      "problem": "$\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}-1+{sin}^{2}⁡x=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x-1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}-1+{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x-1}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}-1+{sin}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following identity: \n\n$$\n1-{sin}^{2}⁡x={cos}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nUsing the above identity, we can further simplify our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x-1}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}-1+{sin}^{2}⁡x & =\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x-1}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}-(1-{sin}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x-1}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}-{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}-\\frac{1}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}-{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{sec}^{2}⁡x}}{\\cancel{{sec}^{2}⁡x}}-\\frac{1}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}-{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}-{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡x-{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1-2{cos}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}-1+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1-2{cos}^{2}⁡x.\n$$",
