    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-43292",
      "knowledge_point_id": "141d61c7-1d82-52fe-b977-19daa910da9b",
      "knowledge_point": "Simplifying an Expression by Factoring Alternative Forms of the Secant-Tangent Identity",
      "problem": "${csc}^{2}⁡\\theta {tan}^{4}⁡\\theta -{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we recall the following identities: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\n$$\n\nUsing the above identity, we can simplify our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta {tan}^{4}⁡\\theta -{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\frac{{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }-\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\cancel{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }}\\frac{\\cancel{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta -{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ({sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can rewrite our expression using $(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ({sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1) & ={tan}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={tan}^{4}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta {tan}^{4}⁡\\theta -{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ={tan}^{4}⁡\\theta .\n$$",
