      "knowledge_point": "Simplifying an Expression by Factoring and Using Various Forms of the Secant-Tangent Identity",
      "problem": "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{4}⁡x+1}?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x-1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{4}⁡x+1} & =\\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}⁡x({sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x)+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}⁡x\\cdot 1+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}⁡x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can use $(1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}⁡x+1} & =\\frac{1}{{sec}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x})}^{2} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{4}⁡x+1}={cos}^{2}⁡x.\n$$",
