      "problem": "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\frac{{tan}^{4}⁡\\theta -{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{tan}^{4}⁡\\theta -{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{{tan}^{4}⁡\\theta -({sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)}{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{{tan}^{4}⁡\\theta -{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can further simplify our expression and use again $(2)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{tan}^{4}⁡\\theta -{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ({tan}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)}{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }({tan}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)}{\\cancel{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & ={tan}^{2}⁡\\theta -1 \\\\  & =({sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)-1 \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{tan}^{4}⁡\\theta -{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -2.\n$$",
